{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_vrtx_01GgCum6mqdWcYD9dyrGyYvo","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Done. Wrote **`ai-engineering/answers/06-002-sql-vs-nosql-for-ai-workloads.md`** — Q2: SQL vs NoSQL for AI workloads.\n\n**Key content:**\n- **Concept:** Choice is component-by-component, not system-wide — different AI components have fundamentally different access patterns\n- **Decision table:** 8 common AI system components mapped to their storage technology (Pinecone/Qdrant for vectors, PostgreSQL for metadata/audit, Redis for online feature serving + semantic cache, Parquet/BigQuery for offline training data)\n- **5-heuristic decision tree:** ACID joins → Postgres; sub-ms point lookups → Redis/DynamoDB; ANN search → vector DB; petabyte analytics → columnar; flexible schema → MongoDB\n- **Concrete RAG stack example:** Pinecone + PostgreSQL + Redis + S3/Parquet, with cost math on semantic caching\n- **Pitfalls:** \"NoSQL = more scalable\" without specificity; pgvector for all workloads without citing its <1M row limit; ignoring caching layers\n- **Cross-links:** Q1 (feature engineering → feature store), Q8 (ingestion architecture), 02-001 (RAG system design)"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":368,"cache_read_input_tokens":68314,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":368,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"62941216-69a3-45ec-b77c-bd9f5f6c778a","uuid":"6731e765-6a43-4c53-b074-e8e1611ecbf5"}
